  "task_completions": [
    "Depending on whether you are looking for neutral, descriptive, or flattering terms, here are several lists of words to describe body shape.\n\n### 1. General & Neutral (Commonly used)\nThese words describe the general silhouette of a person.\n1. **Slim** – Thin in an attractive or healthy way.\n2. **Athletic** – Firm, toned, and looks like they exercise.\n3. **Curvy** – Having a shaped figure with curves (often referring to hips and chest).\n4. **Stocky** – Broad and sturdy; usually shorter and strong.\n5. **Petite** – Small and slender (usually refers to height as well).\n6. **Pear-shaped** – Wider at the hips than the shoulders.\n7. **Apple-shaped** – Carrying more weight around the midsection/stomach.\n8. **Hourglass** – Balanced shoulders and hips with a narrow waist.\n9. **Lanky** – Tall and thin, sometimes moving awkwardly.\n10. **Plump** – Softly rounded; slightly overweight.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Strong & Muscular\nUse these words for people with a lot of muscle mass.\n1. **Toned** – Muscles are defined but not bulky.\n2. **Ripped** – Very low body fat with highly visible muscles.\n3. **Buff** – Large, strong muscles.\n4. **Brawny** – Physically strong and powerful.\n5. **Lean** – Thin but with strong, visible muscle.\n6. **Wiry** – Thin but very strong and tough.\n7. **Burly** – Large, heavy, and strong.\n8. **Solid** – Firm and sturdy.\n9. **Athletic** – Fit and active.\n10. **Husky** – Big and strong (often used for men/boys).\n\n---\n\n### 3. Slender & Thin\nUse these words to describe people with less body mass.\n1. **Slight** – Small and thin in build.\n2. **Slender** – Gracefully thin.\n3. **Willowy** – Tall, thin, and graceful.\n4. **Gaunt** – Very thin, often looking sick or tired.\n5. **Svelte** – Sophisticated, slim, and elegant.\n6. **Scrawny** – Unattractively thin and bony.",
    "Depending on whether you are describing someone in a positive, neutral, or medical way, here are ten common words to describe body shape:\n\n**General & Neutral**\n1. **Athletic** – Strong, fit, and toned (often associated with sports).\n2. **Slender** – Thin in an attractive or graceful way.\n3. **Curvy** – Having a shaped figure with a defined waist and fuller hips or chest.\n4. **Stocky** – Broad and sturdy; usually short but strong.\n5. **Petite** – Small and slender (typically used for women).\n\n**Describing Fullness**\n6. **Plump** – Slightly overweight in a soft, rounded way.\n7. **Lean** – Thin but with visible muscle; very little body fat.\n8. **Husky** – Big and strong (often used for men or boys).\n9. **Voluptuous** – Full-figured and shapely (usually used as a compliment).\n10. **Lanky** – Tall and thin, often moving somewhat awkwardly.",
    "Depending on whether you are describing someone in a medical, artistic, or casual way, the words you use will change. Here are ten words describing body shape, categorized by their tone:\n\n### Neutral/General\n1. **Athletic** – Strong, fit, and often muscular.\n2. **Slender** – Thin in an attractive or graceful way.\n3. **Curvy** – Having a shapely figure with defined curves (usually referring to hips and chest).\n4. **Stocky** – Broad and sturdy; usually shorter and strong.\n5. **Petite** – Small and slender (typically used for women).\n\n### Descriptive/Specific\n6. **Lanky** – Tall and thin, sometimes moving awkwardly.\n7. **Pear-shaped** – Wider at the hips than at the shoulders.\n8. **Hourglass** – A balanced figure where the bust and hips are similar in width with a narrow waist.\n9. **Muscular** – Having well-defined muscles and a powerful build.\n10. **Plump** – Slightly overweight in a soft, rounded way."
  ],
